WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:02.190
Varying wind patterns.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:05.350
Sea levels being higher than you're used to.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:10.170
No less than forth and I can see differences
from when I was a child.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:17.680
Not mitigating CO2 emissions and destroying
our biodiversity, we are killing our planet.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:25.080
Ultimately, the emergency climate comes down
to a single number, the concentration of carbon

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.420
in our atmosphere.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:36.250
The measure that greatly determines global
temperature.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:41.329
Putting a hard number on the economic impacts
of climate change is difficult, it’s difficult

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:47.039
to say that Hurricane Harvey, which caused
125 billion dollars in damages would not have

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:52.300
happened or been less severe without the effects
of climate change in play.It’s difficult

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:58.879
to say that the 2018 wildfires in California,
which caused nearly 150 billion dollars worth

00:00:58.879 --> 00:01:01.350
of damage, were caused by climate change.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:06.730
It’s difficult to say that climate change
caused the severe cold snap of 2021, which

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:13.410
left millions of Texans without power, and
cost the state’s economy upward of 130 billion

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.410
dollars.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:15.410
[1]

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:19.570
But it’s not difficult to say that these
natural disasters have been increasing in

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:25.510
frequency and severity, and the reasons for
this are well understood and documented.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:30.990
Multiple studies, across industries, have
all agreed that climate change driven by human

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:36.740
carbon emissions is going to cause severe
economic unrest, and that’s ignoring the

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.560
obvious human impacts of these natural disasters.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.510
These extreme weather events affect the most
vulnerable people in our societies most.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:46.510
[2]

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:53.400
We need to address this problem now, but finding
ways to accelerate our shift from fossil fuels

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:54.910
has been difficult.

00:01:54.910 --> 00:02:01.760
We depend on fossil fuels to drive our economy,
so not using them to save our economies is

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.520
a catch 22.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.460
These intangible economic impacts are unmotivating
to most industries.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:15.590
We need an immediate way to encourage the
shift by imposing tangible economic pressure.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:20.590
This is where carbon taxes come in, taxes
that are levied directly on the source of

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:26.500
carbon emissions, but this economic policy
comes with its controversies and problems.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:27.500
It’s easy to understand the arguments against
carbon taxes.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.930
Take a simple carbon tax that is placed directly
on fuels according to their carbon emissions.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:38.390
The idea here is to decentivize the use of
carbon intensive fuels, but how would that

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.090
actually play out?

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:45.400
At an individual human level the most obvious
answer is that fuel prices will go up and

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:50.470
make it more expensive for normal people to
get to their jobs and heat their homes.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:56.280
Individuals that can afford new electric vehicles
will buy new electric cars, but the reality

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:02.170
is the vast majority of people can’t afford
to make that switch, and electric car manufacturing

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:06.230
isn’t producing enough vehicles to allow
that immediate change.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:11.330
The people who struggle most financially,
will be pushed even further into poverty.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:15.140
On a larger scale, the economic impacts are
harder to predict.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.670
Electricity prices would of course also be
affected.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:24.340
Our grids rely on fossil fuel for power, and
an increase in fuel cost would be passed onto

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.430
consumers.

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:31.840
But, increased electricity and fuel prices
impact every single industry on the planet.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:38.069
A sudden increase in overhead costs for businesses
would likely cause a dip in countries GDPs

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:40.240
as industries adapt.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.310
These are serious issues that governments
need to consider and weigh up against future

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:50.530
and often intangible economic losses as a
result of climate change.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:55.310
There are so many variables we need to consider
when creating policies like this.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:56.310
What is the optimum pricing of carbon taxes?

00:03:56.310 --> 00:04:01.900
They need to be high enough to make high carbon
intensity fuels like coal really unattractive

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:07.760
and force them out of the market, but they
can’t be so high that they cripple the economy.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.620
What should the revenue be spent on?

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.790
Should the money go back into rebates for
lower economic classes that are going to feel

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:19.959
the pinch most or should it be used as a tax
swap for corporations to reduce the drop in

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:20.959
GDP?

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:26.151
Today, we are going to examine these questions
with the help of two computer models and the

00:04:26.151 --> 00:04:31.660
work of MIT researchers [3] who combined the
two models to predict the future under various

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:33.660
taxation scenarios.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:39.530
The models used were the ReEDS model, developed
by the US Department of Energy to simulate

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:45.871
the electric grid, and the USREP model, developed
by MIT, which simulates the electrical grid's

00:04:45.871 --> 00:04:48.780
effects on the US economy.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.620
Combined these computer models allowed the
researchers to make predictions on how different

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:59.180
policies would affect not just the electrical
grid, but the US economy as a whole.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.590
And the results are pretty fascinating.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.340
Two starting scenarios were tested.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:10.570
Starting in 2020, the carbon tax would start
at either 25 dollars per ton of CO2 or 50

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:12.570
dollars per ton of CO2.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.210
So how does that work?

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.630
How do we measure CO2 release?

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:21.610
One way we can do this is by taxing fuels
according to their carbon dioxide emissions

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:28.840
per million btu, which stands for British
Thermal Unit and I refuse to use it, so let’s

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:34.620
do a quick conversion to the big boy pants
scientific unit of Joules, which more or less

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:37.490
equals 1 gigajoule plus some change.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:41.900
1 gigajoule of coal is only about 34 kilograms
of coal.

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:45.700
[4] Maybe 2 bags worth of your traditional
home heating coal.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:51.271
Coal emits about 0.1 metric tonne of carbon
dioxide per gigajoule.[5] If we priced our

00:05:51.271 --> 00:05:57.320
carbon tax at $50 dollars per metric tonne
that would result in 5 dollars of additional

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:58.320
cost.

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.560
However, we can see that natural gas releases
about half the carbon dioxide that coal does,

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:09.780
making its penalties half that of coal, and
thus giving it a major advantage in the market.

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:10.780
[5]

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.570
Next, these studies applied two annual rates
of carbon tax hikes.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:22.419
One where the tax would increase by 1% a year,
and one where it increased 5% a year.

00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:24.780
Combined this gives us 4 scenarios.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:28.720
Let’s see what happens in these 4 scenarios.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:35.030
The electricity price projections from 2020
through 2050 for each scenario looked like

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:39.460
this.Unsurprisingly electricity prices jumped
in all 4..

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:45.520
With the initial spike being entirely determined
by the starting tax of 25 and 50 dollars per

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:53.190
tonne of CO2, but despite the tax increasing
by 1% or 5% every year, the prices do not

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.140
continually increase.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:07:00.610
The electricity grid adapts and evolves to
the new tax environment very quickly.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:07.150
With the most aggressive tax policy the initial
price spike is 50%, a very large increase,

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:13.340
which decreases to 30% by 2030, and then decreases
again in 2040.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:18.810
These price’s increases are daunting, but
it’s incredible to see how this simulated

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.270
electric grid adapted.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:27.590
This is the energy generation projection for
the entire US under a no tax reference case.

00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:33.090
Coal, natural gas, and nuclear provide the
brunt of the country's energy, with the only

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:38.670
major drivers for change being cheap wind
and solar increasing in generation and being

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:41.930
supported by rapid response natural gas power
plants.

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:44.210
Both eating into coals percentage.

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:48.979
30 years with only a minor increase in renewable
energy.

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:53.160
However, this is what happens under the high
tax scenario.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:57.980
Coal is immediately and unceremoniously yeeted
from the electrical grid.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:02.250
Its high carbon intensity power becomes completely
unviable.

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:08.080
Within a few years the coal power plants are
throttled out of existence, while natural

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:13.590
gas power plants and wind energy quickly taking
over the void left behind.

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:18.490
Solar energy gradually increases in capacity,
but the study does mention that the mix of

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:23.540
solar and wind is highly sensitive to assumptions
made in the model.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:29.180
Most interestingly, in this high tax scenario
a completely new type of technology appears

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:30.780
around 2040.

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:37.389
With that 50 dollar carbon tax increasing
by 5% every year, the carbon tax in 2040 in

00:08:37.389 --> 00:08:44.159
this scenario will be 133 dollars per tonne
of CO2, and at this stage it actually becomes

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:49.379
economical to combine natural gas with direct
carbon capture.

00:08:49.379 --> 00:08:54.630
Direct carbon capture, a technology that absorbs
carbon dioxide directly from the air currently

00:08:54.630 --> 00:09:00.490
costs between 250 to 600 dollars per tonne.
[6] It’s nowhere near cost effective, but

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:06.230
that price is expected to continue falling,
and it will be a lot cheaper to extract carbon

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:12.160
dioxide from the exhaust of a gas turbine
than trying to capture it after it’s diluted

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:13.480
into the atmosphere.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:19.410
And say by 2040 we manage to get the price
down to 100 dollars per tonne, combining this

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:23.649
technology with natural gas plants will make
a lot of sense.

00:09:23.649 --> 00:09:28.790
That’s 33 dollars of cost savings for every
tonne of captured carbon dioxide.

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:34.709
What’s truly encouraging is that under this
scenario carbon dioxide released drops by

00:09:34.709 --> 00:09:37.350
over 90% by 2050.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:42.259
If we plot the other tax scenarios on this
graph this is what we get.

00:09:42.259 --> 00:09:47.980
The big takeaway from this particular graph
is that the 25 dollar at 5% growth is more

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:53.800
effective long term than the 50 dollar at
1%, which makes perfect sense as the high

00:09:53.800 --> 00:10:00.829
growth 25 dollar tax overtakes the low growth
50 dollar tax within 18 years.

00:10:00.829 --> 00:10:05.709
This plays out with our emissions projections
too, with emission reductions stagnating for

00:10:05.709 --> 00:10:07.379
the 1% growth scenarios.

00:10:07.379 --> 00:10:13.850
They just about manage to offset increases
in emissions from economic growth, which is

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:14.850
not enough.

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:20.749
It’s clear a higher rate of increase of
5% is needed to encourage rapid responses

00:10:20.749 --> 00:10:22.490
from industries.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:25.739
All 4 scenarios resulted in coal dying.

00:10:25.739 --> 00:10:31.190
It seems the magic number to make that happen
is around 30-40 dollars per tonne of carbon

00:10:31.190 --> 00:10:37.459
dioxide, and in every scenario, eliminating
coal is what drives the major drops in carbon

00:10:37.459 --> 00:10:44.239
emissions, and we absolutely need to prioritize
getting it out of our energy generation mix.

00:10:44.239 --> 00:10:47.829
So what actually happens to all that money
raised?

00:10:47.829 --> 00:10:52.259
This graph shows the revenue raised under
the 4 different conditions.

00:10:52.259 --> 00:10:56.339
With the high tax high growth scenario raising
over 600 billion dollars.

00:10:56.339 --> 00:10:57.339
That’s a lot of money.

00:10:57.339 --> 00:10:59.269
What should we actually do with this money?

00:10:59.269 --> 00:11:01.480
It doesn’t just vanish.

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:07.309
This study examined 3 primary ways to recycle
the money raised to offset the negative effects

00:11:07.309 --> 00:11:08.679
of the tax.

00:11:08.679 --> 00:11:14.660
Lump sum rebates to households affected by
the carbon tax, labor income tax reductions,

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:21.390
and capital income tax reductions along with
5 hybrids of these 3 potential tax pathways.

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:27.089
They found that direct returns of the money
to households is the most progressive, meaning

00:11:27.089 --> 00:11:30.670
it minimizes the impact to low income households.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:35.850
However, they found that direct capital tax
reductions are the most efficient for maintaining

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:36.850
the economy.

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:42.730
It’s essentially a tax swap, any money raised
from the carbon tax is just used to reduce

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:44.110
capital taxes.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:49.550
But this is the least progressive policy,
favoring richer households.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:55.339
The best policy seems to be a hybrid system,
where 6-8% of the carbon tax is recycled to

00:11:55.339 --> 00:12:01.790
low income houses, while the rest goes directly
to a tax swap with capital tax.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:06.800
This seems like a happy medium between progressivity
and economic efficiency.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:13.110
It’s clear that carbon taxes work, straight
taxation of the fuels is the easiest to understand,

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:18.709
but places like California have implemented
cap and trade systems that put a hard cap

00:12:18.709 --> 00:12:23.660
on carbon emissions with allowances to different
companies.

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:28.360
Companies that manage to reduce their emissions
can then trade that allowance to other companies

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:29.649
that need it.

00:12:29.649 --> 00:12:34.959
This is a much harder system to keep track
of however, as there is no easy way to monitor

00:12:34.959 --> 00:12:37.549
a company's emissions across activities.

00:12:37.549 --> 00:12:42.920
And we have seen with high profile cases like
Volkswagen’s emission cheating technology

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:48.480
that was fitted in 11 million vehicles, that
companies are willing to find ways to cheat

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:50.649
these systems if possible.

00:12:50.649 --> 00:12:55.040
A direct tax on fuels is impossible to cheat.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:56.279
This system works.

00:12:56.279 --> 00:13:01.170
It forces polluters to adapt their business
quickly, it causes a radical reduction in

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:04.439
carbon emissions, and encourages innovation.

00:13:04.439 --> 00:13:09.829
Many countries are implementing carbon taxes
and are gradually ratcheting them up, but

00:13:09.829 --> 00:13:12.079
the United States is not one of them.

00:13:12.079 --> 00:13:17.269
In fact the fossil fuel industry is still
benefiting from massive tax subsidies while

00:13:17.269 --> 00:13:23.170
their own lobbyists fight against carbon tax
with the high IQ argument of “taxes are

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:24.170
bad”.

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:29.989
No-one wants to pay more taxes, but the reality
is this tax can be implemented and result

00:13:29.989 --> 00:13:35.550
no extra tax revenue being raised, it just
shifts where taxes are being pulled from and

00:13:35.550 --> 00:13:42.309
encourages the shift to low carbon energy
that will save us millions of dollars in the

00:13:42.309 --> 00:13:47.550
long run from reducing the impact of climate
change on our planet.

00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:52.449
This is one of the few videos we have made
where we have focused on a single study.

00:13:52.449 --> 00:13:57.109
Videos like this can feel really dry, just
filled with data and graphs.

00:13:57.109 --> 00:14:01.519
We have tried to make subjects like this as
interesting as possible, because it’s incredibly

00:14:01.519 --> 00:14:06.019
important that this information be publicly
known and understood.

00:14:06.019 --> 00:14:10.690
Part of making it interesting is by making
it visually stimulating and there is only

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:15.870
so much you can do with a graph in 2D motion
graphics before it starts to feel like a high

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:18.240
budget powerpoint presentation.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:24.010
Investing in our 3D animation capabilities
was our biggest goal for 2021 and I think

00:14:24.010 --> 00:14:29.619
it's paid off, allowing us to create our little
virtual Real Engineering office in blender.

00:14:29.619 --> 00:14:33.699
Where we can place models of the machines
we talk about and leave easter eggs scattered

00:14:33.699 --> 00:14:37.880
around the room that the vast majority of
ye never notice.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:42.889
Most importantly, it allows us to make the
raw data we talk about a little more visually

00:14:42.889 --> 00:14:44.100
interesting.

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:48.769
Blender is a free software that anyone can
use, but it can be a bit daunting to learn

00:14:48.769 --> 00:14:51.019
a new program like this.

00:14:51.019 --> 00:14:55.879
Luckily there are excellent classes on Skillshare
